# ── Credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
QUICKBOOKS_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id_here
QUICKBOOKS_REFRESH_TOKEN=your_refresh_token_here
QUICKBOOKS_REALM_ID=your_company_id_here

# Sandbox uses a localhost callback. For production use Intuit's hosted
# redirect URI instead — see "Production Setup" in the README.
QUICKBOOKS_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8000/callback
QUICKBOOKS_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox

# ── CLIENT SECRET IS NOT IN THIS FILE — BY DESIGN ───────────────────────
# The bin/ wrappers read it from the macOS Keychain and pass it as an env
# var, so anything that merely reads this file gets a refresh token it
# cannot use (Intuit's token endpoint needs client_id:client_secret).
#
# Create the entry once:
#   security add-generic-password -a "$(id -un)" -s qbo-prod-client-secret -U -w
#
# Do NOT add QUICKBOOKS_CLIENT_SECRET here. quickbooks-client.ts calls
# dotenv with override:true, so a value in this file beats the one the
# wrapper exports — silently defeating the Keychain split.
#
# Not on macOS, or launching node directly instead of via bin/? Then you do
# need QUICKBOOKS_CLIENT_SECRET here, and should accept that the secret and
# the refresh token sit in the same file.

# ── Restriction mode ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Omit or set false to register all tools (default: all registered).
# The bin/qbo wrapper sets all three, which is what makes it read-only.
# QUICKBOOKS_DISABLE_WRITE=true    # suppress create_* and create-* tools
# QUICKBOOKS_DISABLE_UPDATE=true   # suppress update_* and update-* tools
# QUICKBOOKS_DISABLE_DELETE=true   # suppress delete_* and delete-* tools
